notebook_entries.content:
### Field Testing Reality
In lab testing on iPads and Chrome simulated touch displays, our phonics exercise buttons passed all accessibility guidelines. But when we deployed to a public primary school in Nairobi with 20 donated 7-inch Android tablets, half had hairline cracks across the lower digitizer glass.

### The Fix
Instead of requiring users to hit exact visual bounding boxes, we implemented touch slop expansion using CSS pseudo-elements and an invisible 48px hit grid. Even with partial touch latency, tap targets register cleanly.

```css
.phonics-target {
  position: relative;
  min-height: 48px !important;
}
.phonics-target::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -10px;
}
```

Lesson learned: Always test educational software on low-cost and refurbished hardware under actual classroom lighting.

